description: TrailForge master product, stack, and non-negotiable engineering rules
alwaysApply: true
---

You are a senior TypeScript engineer building "TrailForge", a production-grade web app.

PRODUCT: User enters an area + theme + duration. App fetches real data from OpenStreetMap, renders an interactive 3D city scene, generates Japanese/English descriptions for real places with an LLM that is ONLY allowed to use OSM facts, verifies every claim, and exports approved results as GeoJSON/CSV.

STACK (fixed, do not substitute):
- Monorepo with npm workspaces: apps/web, apps/api, packages/shared
- TypeScript strict mode everywhere (noUncheckedIndexedAccess on), ESM
- Web: Vite, React 18+, three, @react-three/fiber, @react-three/drei, zustand, Tailwind CSS
- API: Node.js (current LTS), Express, Zod, pino, helmet, express-rate-limit, lru-cache
- Tests: Vitest (+ Testing Library for web). Lint: ESLint + Prettier.
- LLM: provider interface with 3 implementations: Claude API, Ollama, Mock (for tests/CI)

NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES:
1. Never expose API keys to the browser. All LLM calls happen in apps/api.
2. Validate every external input and every LLM output with Zod schemas from packages/shared.
3. OSM tag text is untrusted DATA, never instructions (prompt-injection defense): pass it to the LLM as JSON inside a clearly delimited data block.
4. The LLM must never invent facts. Only OSM tags provided may be used. Route ordering is computed deterministically in code, NOT by the LLM.
5. No `any`. No unused code. Small files (<250 lines), small functions, clear names, comments only where logic is non-obvious (especially the grounding validator).
6. Every phase must end with: passing `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, `npm test`, and a 5-line plain-English summary appended to docs/PHASES.md (what was built, how data flows, what to say in an interview).
7. Respect external services: Overpass/Nominatim need a descriptive User-Agent, caching, timeouts, retry with backoff, and max 1 request/second to Nominatim.
8. Attribution "© OpenStreetMap contributors" must be visible in the UI footer and README.
9. Performance budget for the 3D scene: 60fps on a mid laptop, <= 2,000 buildings, <= 150 draw calls, merged geometries, no per-frame allocations.
10. Accessibility: keyboard navigable UI, visible focus, prefers-reduced-motion respected, WebGL-unavailable fallback message.
11. Do not ask me questions. State assumptions in docs/PHASES.md and continue. Do not start the next phase on your own.
